1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,843
For over six hundred years, one family sat on a throne that stretched from the

2
00:00:03,897 --> 00:00:06,806
gates of Vienna to the shores of the Persian Gulf.

3
00:00:07,006 --> 00:00:10,970
Sultans. Caliphs. The last dynasty of Islam's greatest

4
00:00:11,039 --> 00:00:14,733
empire. And then, within the span of two winters,

5
00:00:14,773 --> 00:00:19,217
every one of them was given a number of hours to pack a bag and get on a boat or

6
00:00:19,257 --> 00:00:21,459
a train, never to return.

7
00:00:21,620 --> 00:00:25,623
If your family has any connection to this part of the world, or if you've ever

8
00:00:25,667 --> 00:00:29,947
wondered what actually happens to royalty after the empire disappears from under

9
00:00:29,995 --> 00:00:34,511
them, this is the story nobody tells in full: what happened to the people

10
00:00:34,576 --> 00:00:37,394
themselves. The ones who were born into absolute

11
00:00:37,474 --> 00:00:40,437
power and woke up one day as refugees,

12
00:00:40,494 --> 00:00:44,840
unable to find a single country willing to let them keep their own name.

13
00:00:44,881 --> 00:00:48,444
The family's exile didn't start with a law in 1924.

14
00:00:48,497 --> 00:00:53,488
It started two years earlier, with one man, alone, on a British warship.

15
00:00:53,588 --> 00:00:56,211
Mehmed VI Vahideddin became sultan in

16
00:00:56,291 --> 00:00:59,414
July 1918, at the worst possible moment

17
00:00:59,494 --> 00:01:01,736
in six hundred years of Ottoman history.

18
00:01:01,736 --> 00:01:05,579
The empire had already lost the war. British forces occupied Baghdad and

19
00:01:05,635 --> 00:01:09,583
Jerusalem, and within months of his coronation, Allied troops were sitting in

20
00:01:09,645 --> 00:01:13,106
Istanbul itself. Vahideddin's strategy for keeping the

21
00:01:13,166 --> 00:01:17,192
dynasty alive was cooperation. He signed the Treaty of Sèvres in August

22
00:01:17,228 --> 00:01:21,516
1920, a document that stripped the empire down to little more than the Turkish

23
00:01:21,556 --> 00:01:25,119
heartland and recognized foreign mandates over what was left.

24
00:01:25,226 --> 00:01:28,722
It was meant to save the throne. Instead, it turned him into the

25
00:01:28,769 --> 00:01:32,646
nationalist movement's primary enemy, because every concession he made to the

26
00:01:32,726 --> 00:01:37,050
Allies looked, from Ankara, like a sultan selling out his own country to keep his

27
00:01:37,130 --> 00:01:41,133
palace. By 1922, Mustafa Kemal's forces had

28
00:01:41,186 --> 00:01:45,577
defeated the Greek army in Anatolia and were in firm control of the country.

29
00:01:45,697 --> 00:01:49,941
On November 1, the Grand National Assembly voted to abolish the sultanate

30
00:01:50,021 --> 00:01:54,826
itself, the political office, not yet the family's religious title as Caliph.

31
00:01:54,826 --> 00:01:59,710
Sixteen days later, on November 17, Vahideddin left Constantinople aboard the

32
00:01:59,750 --> 00:02:02,433
British warship HMS Malaya, accompanied

33
00:02:02,486 --> 00:02:06,196
by his ten-year-old son, Prince Ertuğrul, and two of his consorts.

34
00:02:06,329 --> 00:02:08,918
He took almost nothing else. No treasury.

35
00:02:08,998 --> 00:02:13,322
No jewels. Whatever fit in his own hands and the small amount of luggage the British

36
00:02:13,382 --> 00:02:17,967
allowed. The British wouldn't even let him live in a Muslim-majority country, and that alone

37
00:02:18,020 --> 00:02:20,449
shows how fast his status had collapsed.

38
00:02:20,529 --> 00:02:23,131
He asked for Hejaz. He asked for Egypt.

39
00:02:23,171 --> 00:02:26,935
He asked for Palestine. Every request was refused, out of fear

40
00:02:26,967 --> 00:02:30,298
that a deposed Ottoman sultan sitting anywhere in the Islamic world would

41
00:02:30,332 --> 00:02:34,221
become a rallying point for exactly the kind of nationalist unrest the Allies

42
00:02:34,253 --> 00:02:37,985
were trying to suppress elsewhere. He was moved first to Malta, then

43
00:02:38,029 --> 00:02:42,389
eventually given refuge in Italy by King Victor Emmanuel III, and settled in the

44
00:02:42,429 --> 00:02:45,672
small resort town of San Remo on the Italian Riviera.

45
00:02:45,672 --> 00:02:49,915
He tried once more to salvage some version of his old religious authority,

46
00:02:49,951 --> 00:02:54,079
attempting to have himself installed as Caliph in the Hejaz instead, a bid that

47
00:02:54,135 --> 00:02:58,243
collapsed almost immediately under regional rivalry and colonial pressure.

48
00:02:58,403 --> 00:03:01,126
Back in Constantinople, his own cousin,

49
00:03:01,179 --> 00:03:05,369
Abdulmecid, was named Caliph in his place within two days of his departure.

50
00:03:05,509 --> 00:03:09,533
The family kept its religious title even as it stripped the deposed sultan of

51
00:03:09,577 --> 00:03:13,136
everything else. Vahideddin spent his last years in San

52
00:03:13,196 --> 00:03:16,499
Remo writing memoirs nobody in power was listening to.

53
00:03:16,687 --> 00:03:20,983
He died there on May 16, 1926, of a heart condition.

54
00:03:21,075 --> 00:03:23,145
Turkey refused to take the body.

55
00:03:23,225 --> 00:03:27,069
It was his own daughter, Sabiha Sultan, who arranged for him to be buried in

56
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:32,033
Damascus: a sultan of six centuries of Ottoman rule, laid to rest in a cemetery

57
00:03:32,065 --> 00:03:35,557
that wasn't even in his own country, because the country he'd ruled wouldn't

58
00:03:35,589 --> 00:03:39,480
have him back even dead. Two days after Vahideddin sailed away on

59
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:43,804
a British warship, the family handed its last remaining title to a man who never

60
00:03:43,838 --> 00:03:46,847
wanted a throne in the first place. He wanted a studio.

61
00:03:46,967 --> 00:03:49,649
Abdulmecid had spent most of his life waiting.

62
00:03:49,729 --> 00:03:53,733
Under Ottoman custom, a prince of his rank was confined to the palace grounds

63
00:03:53,773 --> 00:03:57,176
until the age of forty, watching three different cousins take the throne ahead

64
00:03:57,203 --> 00:04:01,100
of him while he filled the years with painting lessons, cello practice, and

65
00:04:01,157 --> 00:04:03,422
Friday visits from the artists he supported.

66
00:04:03,502 --> 00:04:07,666
He chaired the Ottoman Artists' Society, studied under European-trained painters,

67
00:04:07,706 --> 00:04:12,390
and produced canvases, portraits, battle scenes, harem interiors, good enough that

68
00:04:12,470 --> 00:04:16,233
a full museum retrospective would eventually be built around them, decades

69
00:04:16,286 --> 00:04:19,116
after his death, in a country that had exiled him.

70
00:04:19,169 --> 00:04:21,999
He became crown prince only in 1918, when

71
00:04:22,039 --> 00:04:25,922
Vahideddin took the throne, and even then he had no real power.

72
00:04:25,975 --> 00:04:28,725
He simply outranked everyone else left standing.

73
00:04:28,725 --> 00:04:32,008
On November 19, 1922, two days after his

74
00:04:32,042 --> 00:04:35,531
cousin's departure, the Grand National Assembly elected him Caliph.

75
00:04:35,638 --> 00:04:40,095
He was chosen by vote, not inheritance: elected by a secular parliament that had

76
00:04:40,143 --> 00:04:43,618
just abolished the political half of his family's power and kept only the

77
00:04:43,666 --> 00:04:47,942
religious half, on its own terms. He arrived in Constantinople five days

78
00:04:47,995 --> 00:04:51,385
later to a position that had been deliberately hollowed out in advance.

79
00:04:51,518 --> 00:04:56,510
No army, no state, no sultanate to back it, just the ceremonial headship of Sunni

80
00:04:56,590 --> 00:05:01,074
Islam operating inside a republic that no longer wanted a monarchy of any kind.

81
00:05:01,207 --> 00:05:03,236
He held it for sixteen months.

82
00:05:03,289 --> 00:05:07,159
In that time he tried, quietly and without much success, to expand the

83
00:05:07,216 --> 00:05:11,003
office back into something resembling real authority, corresponding with Muslim

84
00:05:11,043 --> 00:05:14,526
leaders abroad, hosting foreign dignitaries with more ceremony than the

85
00:05:14,566 --> 00:05:18,369
new government was comfortable with. It was a losing effort from the start,

86
00:05:18,389 --> 00:05:21,332
and on March 3, 1924, the Assembly

87
00:05:21,388 --> 00:05:25,896
abolished the caliphate outright and voted his own family into exile along

88
00:05:25,944 --> 00:05:28,619
with him. He was given almost no warning.

89
00:05:28,739 --> 00:05:32,943
Abdulmecid didn't wait for the three-day grace period the law technically allowed

90
00:05:32,987 --> 00:05:37,267
everyone else; he and his household were put on a train before the law had even

91
00:05:37,320 --> 00:05:39,749
taken effect, at Çatalca rather than

92
00:05:39,811 --> 00:05:44,553
Istanbul's main station, specifically to avoid the kind of public farewell that

93
00:05:44,606 --> 00:05:49,037
might turn into a demonstration. A Jewish stationmaster at Çatalca was

94
00:05:49,088 --> 00:05:53,121
reportedly the last person in the country to treat him with the courtesy of his old

95
00:05:53,201 --> 00:05:57,365
rank before the train pulled out. He settled first in Switzerland, then in

96
00:05:57,461 --> 00:06:02,409
Nice, and eventually in Paris, where he kept painting almost until the end, a

97
00:06:02,469 --> 00:06:05,052
caliph with no caliphate, still signing

98
00:06:05,116 --> 00:06:09,776
canvases the same way he had as a young prince confined to Dolmabahçe.

99
00:06:09,856 --> 00:06:14,180
He wrote twelve volumes of memoirs that his daughter kept safe for decades.

100
00:06:14,180 --> 00:06:16,742
He died in his Paris home on August 23,

101
00:06:16,778 --> 00:06:19,865
1944, of a heart attack, in a city still

102
00:06:19,918 --> 00:06:24,749
under German occupation, an ocean away from either of the two institutions he'd

103
00:06:24,813 --> 00:06:28,993
once briefly headed. Turkey wouldn't take his body any more

104
00:06:29,025 --> 00:06:32,196
than it had taken Vahideddin's eighteen years earlier.

105
00:06:32,316 --> 00:06:37,081
His daughter petitioned for years to have him buried at home and was refused every

106
00:06:37,161 --> 00:06:40,604
time. His coffin sat inside the Grand Mosque of

107
00:06:40,684 --> 00:06:44,127
Paris for a decade, an actual decade, not

108
00:06:44,167 --> 00:06:48,931
a delay of months, while the family tried and failed to find him any resting place

109
00:06:48,988 --> 00:06:52,935
closer to Istanbul than the one his cousin had already been given.

110
00:06:53,255 --> 00:06:57,339
In 1954, ten years after his death, he

111
00:06:57,379 --> 00:07:01,903
was finally interred in Medina, the second man in this family in twenty years

112
00:07:01,956 --> 00:07:05,666
to end up buried in someone else's country because his own wouldn't have

113
00:07:05,726 --> 00:07:10,230
him. By 1924, the empire itself was gone in

114
00:07:10,298 --> 00:07:14,314
every way that mattered, and the one office the family still held, the

115
00:07:14,374 --> 00:07:19,038
caliphate, sitting now with Abdulmecid, was the last thing standing.

116
00:07:19,167 --> 00:07:24,163
That didn't last either. On March 3, 1924, the Turkish parliament

117
00:07:24,219 --> 00:07:28,087
abolished the caliphate outright, and went much further than removing one man

118
00:07:28,135 --> 00:07:32,731
from one office. A hundred and fifty-six members of the House of Osman were stripped of their

119
00:07:32,781 --> 00:07:36,495
Turkish citizenship in a single stroke, and ordered out of the country within

120
00:07:36,535 --> 00:07:40,659
three days. Once you counted the children who left with their parents, the relatives who

121
00:07:40,686 --> 00:07:44,663
weren't technically named in the law but went anyway, and the servants who refused

122
00:07:44,690 --> 00:07:48,666
to abandon the families they'd served for decades, the real number of people forced

123
00:07:48,726 --> 00:07:52,670
out ran into the hundreds. This wasn't just Abdulmecid, the one man

124
00:07:52,710 --> 00:07:56,914
holding the last symbolic title. It was children, elderly widows, distant

125
00:07:56,974 --> 00:07:59,396
cousins who'd never held office in their lives.

126
00:07:59,416 --> 00:08:03,960
The law didn't distinguish between the head of the family and a relative six times removed.

127
00:08:03,960 --> 00:08:07,563
If your surname connected you to the dynasty, you were leaving, and you had

128
00:08:07,623 --> 00:08:11,727
exactly one year to sell whatever property you owned inside Turkey, or lose

129
00:08:11,754 --> 00:08:15,891
it to the state treasury outright. The law even forbade family members from

130
00:08:15,941 --> 00:08:19,814
passing through Turkish territory in transit, on their way to anywhere else.

131
00:08:19,942 --> 00:08:22,457
Bank accounts were frozen the same week.

132
00:08:22,521 --> 00:08:25,499
Passports were stamped for exile, not return.

133
00:08:25,600 --> 00:08:29,103
Six hundred years of rule, and the family's last physical connection to the

134
00:08:29,172 --> 00:08:33,907
empire ended on a rural train platform, in the dark, with an armed escort making

135
00:08:33,939 --> 00:08:37,831
sure they actually got on board. While the family itself was still finding

136
00:08:37,871 --> 00:08:41,834
its footing in Nice and Beirut, the government back in Istanbul moved fast on

137
00:08:41,874 --> 00:08:43,676
the buildings they'd left behind.

138
00:08:43,783 --> 00:08:46,959
On April 3, 1924, barely a month after

139
00:08:46,999 --> 00:08:51,603
the exile law passed, Topkapı Palace, the sultans' primary residence for nearly

140
00:08:51,683 --> 00:08:55,767
four centuries, was converted by government decree into Turkey's first

141
00:08:55,811 --> 00:08:58,890
national museum. The imperial harem quarters the family

142
00:08:58,930 --> 00:09:03,534
had lived and died in for generations became a ticketed exhibition space.

143
00:09:03,614 --> 00:09:08,098
The treasury rooms, sealed for centuries under a ceremony dating back to Sultan

144
00:09:08,178 --> 00:09:10,980
Selim I, were unlocked for the general public.

145
00:09:11,079 --> 00:09:15,465
Visitors could now walk past the same jeweled objects, including the Topkapı

146
00:09:15,534 --> 00:09:19,468
Dagger and the Spoonmaker's Diamond, that had once required a sultan's personal

147
00:09:19,516 --> 00:09:22,511
authority to view. The timing wasn't incidental.

148
00:09:22,611 --> 00:09:26,515
The new republic wanted the empire's former splendor visibly stripped of its

149
00:09:26,584 --> 00:09:31,399
owners and handed to "the nation" as fast as possible, a public, physical

150
00:09:31,456 --> 00:09:35,483
demonstration that the family's exile wasn't just a legal formality.

151
00:09:35,543 --> 00:09:39,566
Dolmabahçe Palace, the newer Bosphorus residence the family had actually been

152
00:09:39,612 --> 00:09:43,650
living in more recently, followed the same path in the years after, eventually

153
00:09:43,686 --> 00:09:47,253
becoming a museum and occasional state residence in its own right.

154
00:09:47,253 --> 00:09:51,017
The family that had once required a formal ceremony just to unlock their own

155
00:09:51,079 --> 00:09:55,180
treasury now had less right to walk through those rooms than any tourist

156
00:09:55,220 --> 00:09:59,745
holding a ticket. The same March 3, 1924 sitting of

157
00:09:59,789 --> 00:10:04,068
parliament that abolished the caliphate also created an entirely new government

158
00:10:04,148 --> 00:10:08,392
body, the Directorate General of Foundations, and handed it control of

159
00:10:08,459 --> 00:10:12,236
every vakıf in the country, the centuries-old religious endowments that

160
00:10:12,276 --> 00:10:16,880
had funded mosques, soup kitchens, schools and hospitals across the empire,

161
00:10:17,008 --> 00:10:21,844
many of them established and personally funded by sultans going back generations.

162
00:10:21,965 --> 00:10:26,088
Overnight, an entire category of family-linked property that had

163
00:10:26,121 --> 00:10:29,692
technically never belonged to any individual sultan, but had always been

164
00:10:29,752 --> 00:10:33,695
managed through the family's religious authority, was folded into the state and

165
00:10:33,752 --> 00:10:38,259
placed permanently outside anything a descendant could ever claim as inheritance.

166
00:10:38,479 --> 00:10:42,823
The shockwaves from that one law didn't stop at Turkey's borders.

167
00:10:42,923 --> 00:10:45,466
The Ottoman sultan hadn't just been a monarch.

168
00:10:45,466 --> 00:10:49,630
As Caliph, he was recognized across the Muslim world as the spiritual head of

169
00:10:49,697 --> 00:10:53,313
Sunni Islam. Abolishing the title didn't just end a

170
00:10:53,383 --> 00:10:57,877
dynasty. It ended an institution millions of people outside Turkey considered

171
00:10:57,933 --> 00:11:02,441
essential to their own religious life, and the reaction proved it within weeks.

172
00:11:02,601 --> 00:11:04,603
Nowhere felt it harder than India.

173
00:11:04,803 --> 00:11:08,206
Since 1919, Indian Muslims had run an

174
00:11:08,275 --> 00:11:12,530
entire political movement, the Khilafat Movement, built around defending the

175
00:11:12,580 --> 00:11:15,333
Ottoman caliphate from exactly this outcome.

176
00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:18,295
Under the Ali brothers, Muhammad Ali and

177
00:11:18,348 --> 00:11:20,778
Shaukat Ali, the movement had organized

178
00:11:20,838 --> 00:11:25,582
boycotts of British goods, mass protests, and an alliance with Gandhi's Indian

179
00:11:25,626 --> 00:11:29,586
National Congress that produced one of the first real moments of Hindu-Muslim

180
00:11:29,634 --> 00:11:31,828
political unity under British rule.

181
00:11:31,965 --> 00:11:36,792
Gandhi himself folded the cause into his non-cooperation campaign, telling

182
00:11:36,836 --> 00:11:40,636
supporters that defending the caliphate and defending Indian self-rule were the

183
00:11:40,700 --> 00:11:45,440
same fight. Millions of Indian Muslims had spent five years, and no small amount of their own

184
00:11:45,490 --> 00:11:48,323
freedom, fighting to keep this one office intact.

185
00:11:48,435 --> 00:11:52,807
Then Turkey abolished it from the inside, on its own initiative, with no outside

186
00:11:52,851 --> 00:11:57,130
pressure forcing the decision. The Indian scholar Maulana Mohammad Ali

187
00:11:57,211 --> 00:12:01,775
Jauhar's reaction was somewhere close to grief: he called it the greatest betrayal

188
00:12:01,802 --> 00:12:06,179
in Islamic history, accusing the Turks of destroying the very institution Indian

189
00:12:06,239 --> 00:12:10,903
Muslims had spent years defending, at real personal cost, on Turkey's behalf.

190
00:12:11,023 --> 00:12:14,586
The movement that had briefly united Hindus and Muslims against the British

191
00:12:14,634 --> 00:12:18,430
collapsed within months, robbed of the one cause that had held it together.

192
00:12:18,521 --> 00:12:22,593
Within a few years, that fragile alliance between Congress and the Khilafat

193
00:12:22,638 --> 00:12:27,318
leadership had broken down entirely, and Hindu-Muslim relations in India hardened

194
00:12:27,371 --> 00:12:30,200
along lines that would matter again two decades later.

195
00:12:30,328 --> 00:12:33,964
Elsewhere, the reaction was less grief and more opportunism.

196
00:12:33,964 --> 00:12:37,967
With the office suddenly vacant, several rulers quietly floated themselves as the

197
00:12:38,007 --> 00:12:41,491
new Caliph. Egypt's King Fuad I let it be known he

198
00:12:41,531 --> 00:12:44,854
might be willing to take up the title, and reportedly funded scholars

199
00:12:44,901 --> 00:12:49,097
sympathetic to his own claim. In the Hejaz, King Hussein went further

200
00:12:49,157 --> 00:12:53,261
and simply declared himself Caliph outright within weeks of the abolition,

201
00:12:53,272 --> 00:12:56,784
though the claim never gained meaningful recognition beyond his own court and

202
00:12:56,840 --> 00:12:59,427
collapsed along with his kingdom the following year.

203
00:12:59,534 --> 00:13:04,151
In 1926, Muslim scholars convened a conference in Cairo specifically to

204
00:13:04,185 --> 00:13:08,555
settle the question, weighing candidates that included Fuad, Hussein, Morocco's

205
00:13:08,601 --> 00:13:12,118
Sultan Moulay Yusef, and India's own Aga Khan III.

206
00:13:12,165 --> 00:13:16,002
After genuine debate, the conference reached no agreement at all, concluding

207
00:13:16,018 --> 00:13:20,886
that reviving the caliphate wasn't realistically possible under the political conditions of the time.

208
00:13:20,886 --> 00:13:24,810
The position that Indian Muslims had fought five years to protect, and that

209
00:13:24,858 --> 00:13:28,973
half a dozen rulers had quietly hoped to claim for themselves, simply stayed

210
00:13:29,030 --> 00:13:32,537
vacant. In the century since, it still has.

211
00:13:32,657 --> 00:13:36,981
Exile is easy to say and hard to actually live through, and the family found that

212
00:13:37,041 --> 00:13:39,943
out within months. Most of them scattered to whichever

213
00:13:40,005 --> 00:13:42,426
European city would issue them a visa:

214
00:13:42,506 --> 00:13:46,389
Nice, Beirut, Alexandria, later Paris and Vienna.

215
00:13:46,529 --> 00:13:49,392
A few had savings or jewelry they'd managed to carry out.

216
00:13:49,520 --> 00:13:53,235
Most didn't. Some lived off the charity of wealthier relatives.

217
00:13:53,363 --> 00:13:58,120
Some took ordinary jobs under shortened or assumed names, quietly dropping any

218
00:13:58,187 --> 00:14:01,643
trace of the family name from their paperwork, in countries that had no

219
00:14:01,694 --> 00:14:05,807
particular reason to be sympathetic to a deposed Muslim dynasty in the interwar

220
00:14:05,900 --> 00:14:08,849
years. A handful of sympathetic outsiders tried

221
00:14:08,876 --> 00:14:11,332
to help: the ruler of Hyderabad, an

222
00:14:11,385 --> 00:14:16,056
Egyptian prince, even the King of Hejaz sent money at various points, but with

223
00:14:16,076 --> 00:14:20,140
the family scattered across a dozen countries, most of that aid simply never

224
00:14:20,190 --> 00:14:24,544
reached the people who needed it most. France, for its part, wouldn't grant

225
00:14:24,597 --> 00:14:28,547
citizenship even to descendants of Suleiman the Magnificent, the sultan who

226
00:14:28,587 --> 00:14:31,270
had once come to a French king's rescue militarily.

227
00:14:31,470 --> 00:14:34,873
The best they got was a travel document, not a passport.

228
00:14:35,020 --> 00:14:39,917
Abdulmecid, the last Caliph, had already spent his final two decades painting and

229
00:14:39,958 --> 00:14:44,722
writing memoirs in Nice and then Paris, largely withdrawn from public life.

230
00:14:44,822 --> 00:14:49,766
His death in 1944, and the decade his coffin spent waiting in a Paris mosque

231
00:14:49,812 --> 00:14:54,090
before Turkey would even permit a distant burial in Medina, was only the sharpest

232
00:14:54,140 --> 00:14:58,734
version of a fate the rest of the family lived out more quietly, one ordinary year

233
00:14:58,761 --> 00:15:01,697
at a time. Every account of this exile tends to

234
00:15:01,750 --> 00:15:06,341
focus on the sultans, the caliphs, the princes with titles worth writing about.

235
00:15:06,432 --> 00:15:10,265
Almost nobody tells you what happened to the people who'd spent their entire lives

236
00:15:10,315 --> 00:15:14,268
serving them, and the record that does exist is thin on purpose.

237
00:15:14,360 --> 00:15:18,672
Nobody was keeping careful files on servants in 1924.

238
00:15:18,692 --> 00:15:21,475
The imperial harem wasn't just a private residence.

239
00:15:21,502 --> 00:15:25,959
It was an enormous administrative institution with its own budgets, ranks,

240
00:15:25,999 --> 00:15:29,963
and internal hierarchy, staffed by hundreds of women, many of them

241
00:15:30,014 --> 00:15:34,367
originally brought in as enslaved girls from the Caucasus and educated within the

242
00:15:34,413 --> 00:15:36,609
palace system for the rest of their lives.

243
00:15:36,737 --> 00:15:41,413
Some rose to genuine positions of influence inside that hierarchy, running

244
00:15:41,478 --> 00:15:45,737
households, training younger women, managing the domestic machinery of an

245
00:15:45,817 --> 00:15:49,020
empire from inside rooms the public never saw.

246
00:15:49,020 --> 00:15:53,344
When the March 1924 law hit, it didn't distinguish between a princess by blood

247
00:15:53,404 --> 00:15:56,947
and a woman who'd spent forty years running a palace household with no legal

248
00:15:56,997 --> 00:15:59,750
freedom of her own. Both were told to leave.

249
00:15:59,790 --> 00:16:02,392
Neither was given anywhere in particular to go.

250
00:16:02,442 --> 00:16:05,835
Turkish government archives from the following year record roughly thirty

251
00:16:05,888 --> 00:16:09,518
enslaved men and women expelled from the palace and quietly redirected into

252
00:16:09,554 --> 00:16:13,602
government institutions as ordinary servants, with no severance and no say in

253
00:16:13,622 --> 00:16:18,246
the reassignment. Contemporary accounts describe many of the harem's former women being sent to

254
00:16:18,326 --> 00:16:22,170
Darülaceze, the state poorhouse in Istanbul, the same institution that

255
00:16:22,216 --> 00:16:25,773
housed the city's homeless and destitute, so that women who had once managed the

256
00:16:25,826 --> 00:16:29,536
domestic affairs of an empire ended their lives in a building built for people with

257
00:16:29,576 --> 00:16:32,659
nothing at all. Two eunuchs expelled from Yıldız Palace

258
00:16:32,712 --> 00:16:37,464
as early as 1909, well before the main exile, were reportedly still living in a

259
00:16:37,517 --> 00:16:40,907
shanty house more than a decade later, forgotten by everyone except the

260
00:16:40,954 --> 00:16:44,430
journalists who occasionally tracked them down for a human-interest piece on the

261
00:16:44,499 --> 00:16:49,154
empire's last remnants. The republican press of 1924 wasn't

262
00:16:49,207 --> 00:16:52,837
sympathetic to any of this. One popular magazine described the newly

263
00:16:52,888 --> 00:16:56,841
emptied palaces as treasure houses built on the blood of the poor, and used the

264
00:16:56,881 --> 00:17:00,524
harem's dissolution as a symbol of everything the new Turkey was supposedly

265
00:17:00,564 --> 00:17:04,448
leaving behind, a narrative that made the women's expulsion into a kind of moral

266
00:17:04,518 --> 00:17:08,452
cleanup rather than what it actually was for most of them: the sudden loss of the

267
00:17:08,500 --> 00:17:10,614
only home and profession they'd ever had.

268
00:17:10,721 --> 00:17:15,178
Whatever the truth of that framing, the individual women themselves rarely got

269
00:17:15,218 --> 00:17:17,500
any say in how their own story was told.

270
00:17:17,500 --> 00:17:21,744
They simply lost the only life they'd ever known, on the same trains and the

271
00:17:21,808 --> 00:17:26,228
same week as the family they'd served, with nobody writing their names down.

272
00:17:26,388 --> 00:17:30,471
A century later, nobody has gone back to write them down since.

273
00:17:30,605 --> 00:17:34,395
If you want to understand what this exile did to someone who actually did have a

274
00:17:34,459 --> 00:17:39,119
name history remembers, there's no better case than Abdulmecid's own granddaughter.

275
00:17:39,279 --> 00:17:41,762
Neslişah Sultan was three years old when

276
00:17:41,782 --> 00:17:45,365
the 1924 law put her family on that train out of Istanbul.

277
00:17:45,505 --> 00:17:49,689
She grew up in Nice, one of hundreds of children in the family learning French

278
00:17:49,729 --> 00:17:53,773
instead of Ottoman court etiquette, her family reportedly struggling with real

279
00:17:53,829 --> 00:17:57,896
financial hardship even as she carried the title of imperial princess.

280
00:17:57,966 --> 00:18:01,620
She had no idea whether she'd ever see the city she was born in again.

281
00:18:01,700 --> 00:18:05,463
In 1941, she married Prince Abdel Moneim,

282
00:18:05,523 --> 00:18:10,187
son of Egypt's last Khedive, a match that, on paper, restored some of what

283
00:18:10,267 --> 00:18:14,952
exile had taken. She was marrying back into royalty, into a family that still actually had a

284
00:18:14,992 --> 00:18:19,476
throne. Eleven years later, that throne collapsed

285
00:18:19,556 --> 00:18:24,520
too. In July 1952, Egypt's Free Officers forced King Farouk to abdicate.

286
00:18:24,660 --> 00:18:28,604
Neslişah's husband was appointed regent for the country's infant king, and for

287
00:18:28,668 --> 00:18:33,088
nine months, she stood in as Egypt's de facto first lady, attending the opera,

288
00:18:33,118 --> 00:18:37,012
running charity hospitals and the Red Crescent, representing a monarchy both of

289
00:18:37,044 --> 00:18:39,173
them must have known was already finished.

290
00:18:39,281 --> 00:18:43,818
In June 1953, the officers abolished the Egyptian monarchy outright.

291
00:18:43,930 --> 00:18:47,421
Four years after that, in 1957, Neslişah

292
00:18:47,441 --> 00:18:49,823
and her husband were arrested and accused of treason.

293
00:18:49,963 --> 00:18:53,426
She was exiled a second time, from a country that had only ever been her

294
00:18:53,473 --> 00:18:57,110
replacement home in the first place. She was released from prison after

295
00:18:57,156 --> 00:19:00,713
Turkey's own president personally intervened on her behalf, the same

296
00:19:00,763 --> 00:19:04,717
Turkish state that had exiled her family thirty-three years earlier, now the one

297
00:19:04,777 --> 00:19:08,720
negotiating for her freedom. She eventually made it back to Istanbul,

298
00:19:08,760 --> 00:19:11,202
the city she'd left as a three-year-old.

299
00:19:11,242 --> 00:19:14,085
She died there in 2012, at ninety-one, in

300
00:19:14,125 --> 00:19:18,249
a villa on the Bosphorus, having lived through the fall of two empires from the

301
00:19:18,318 --> 00:19:22,413
inside of both, and having spent a lifetime, by her biographer's account,

302
00:19:22,520 --> 00:19:26,256
never once relinquishing her own sense of who she was.

303
00:19:26,476 --> 00:19:31,060
Neslişah wasn't the only child of this family who grew up carrying a title with

304
00:19:31,140 --> 00:19:35,224
no country attached to it. A generation later, a different branch

305
00:19:35,268 --> 00:19:39,148
produced its own version of the same story, and it turned out very

306
00:19:39,195 --> 00:19:42,751
differently. Şehzade Ertuğrul Osman, the New York

307
00:19:42,815 --> 00:19:44,753
financier who became head of the house in

308
00:19:44,797 --> 00:19:47,876
1994 and didn't set foot in Turkey until

309
00:19:47,929 --> 00:19:51,799
he was nearly eighty, married twice and had no children of his own.

310
00:19:51,979 --> 00:19:54,842
His story ends, in that sense, exactly

311
00:19:54,869 --> 00:19:59,646
where his family's exile left it: a name with nobody left to carry it forward from

312
00:19:59,686 --> 00:20:02,849
his own line. But other branches of the family, less

313
00:20:02,929 --> 00:20:05,572
famous, kept having children who grew up

314
00:20:05,629 --> 00:20:10,376
asking the same question Neslişah had asked as a three-year-old on a train out

315
00:20:10,403 --> 00:20:15,260
of Istanbul: what does it mean to be a princess when the empire that made you

316
00:20:15,340 --> 00:20:18,703
one has been gone since before your parents were born?

317
00:20:18,823 --> 00:20:23,508
Abdulhamid Kayıhan Osmanoğlu, a great-great-grandson of Sultan Abdul

318
00:20:23,568 --> 00:20:26,470
Hamid II, spent decades as the family's

319
00:20:26,514 --> 00:20:30,954
unofficial spokesman inside Turkey, the one who confirmed deaths and successions

320
00:20:31,007 --> 00:20:34,718
to Turkish reporters when the family association wanted something said

321
00:20:34,780 --> 00:20:38,962
publicly but didn't want the head of the house himself making statements to the

322
00:20:39,055 --> 00:20:42,725
press. It's a strange kind of inherited job: no

323
00:20:42,805 --> 00:20:45,528
throne, no ministry, just a standing

324
00:20:45,592 --> 00:20:49,691
responsibility to explain a dynasty to a country that abolished it.

325
00:20:49,811 --> 00:20:53,054
The pattern repeats down through the youngest generation too.

326
00:20:53,215 --> 00:20:57,779
Harun Osman's own granddaughter, born decades after the exile law was even

327
00:20:57,841 --> 00:21:01,942
repealed, grew up in Istanbul with no memory of statelessness at all.

328
00:21:01,942 --> 00:21:05,866
She never had to learn a foreign language to survive, never watched her family's

329
00:21:05,914 --> 00:21:09,789
bank accounts get frozen. What she inherited instead was a surname

330
00:21:09,822 --> 00:21:13,793
that still draws television cameras, wedding invitations from other deposed

331
00:21:13,833 --> 00:21:18,037
royal houses, and a public curiosity about what an actual Ottoman princess

332
00:21:18,077 --> 00:21:22,281
looks like doing something as ordinary as walking down a street in the city her

333
00:21:22,327 --> 00:21:25,163
family once ruled from a palace two miles away.

334
00:21:25,233 --> 00:21:29,567
Between Neslişah's generation and this one sits the entire shape of what exile

335
00:21:29,611 --> 00:21:32,770
actually cost. One generation lost a country.

336
00:21:32,890 --> 00:21:35,573
The next generation grew up in someone else's.

337
00:21:35,653 --> 00:21:39,576
This generation was simply born already belonging to the place their family had

338
00:21:39,592 --> 00:21:43,740
been dragged out of: proof that a hundred years is long enough for even a forced

339
00:21:43,820 --> 00:21:48,064
exile to fully heal into something that looks, from the outside, like it was

340
00:21:48,104 --> 00:21:51,347
never broken at all. Not every branch of the family handled

341
00:21:51,414 --> 00:21:55,190
exile the same way, and the differences say a lot about what this collapse

342
00:21:55,235 --> 00:21:59,274
actually cost people. Take Şehzade Mehmed Abdülkerim, a

343
00:21:59,345 --> 00:22:01,917
grandson of Sultan Abdülhamid II.

344
00:22:02,037 --> 00:22:06,561
Stripped of a throne, he found a second, stranger use for his royal blood.

345
00:22:06,614 --> 00:22:11,045
In the early 1930s, Japan's government recruited him as a figurehead to incite

346
00:22:11,098 --> 00:22:16,089
an uprising among Turkic Muslims in East Turkestan against Chinese rule, betting

347
00:22:16,105 --> 00:22:20,093
that an actual Ottoman prince's name could rally a rebellion Tokyo wanted for

348
00:22:20,153 --> 00:22:23,416
its own regional interests. The uprising failed.

349
00:22:23,476 --> 00:22:26,018
Japan abandoned him the moment he stopped being useful.

350
00:22:26,151 --> 00:22:31,143
He fled to the United States and, in 1935, took his own life in New York City.

351
00:22:31,276 --> 00:22:35,787
He's buried in a cemetery in Queens, a long way from either Istanbul or East

352
00:22:35,827 --> 00:22:38,910
Turkestan. He left behind two young sons, born in

353
00:22:38,954 --> 00:22:42,513
1930 and 1932, suddenly fatherless in a

354
00:22:42,563 --> 00:22:44,995
country that had no obligation to them at all.

355
00:22:45,055 --> 00:22:47,237
One of those boys, Harun, eventually

356
00:22:47,282 --> 00:22:52,202
returned to Istanbul once the 1974 law finally allowed men of the dynasty back

357
00:22:52,234 --> 00:22:55,245
into the country. He is, as of today, the current head of

358
00:22:55,265 --> 00:22:59,168
the House of Osman, a position he holds not because of anything resembling a

359
00:22:59,237 --> 00:23:03,252
throne, but because a genealogical chart maintained by a private family

360
00:23:03,319 --> 00:23:05,934
association says he's next in line.

361
00:23:06,074 --> 00:23:10,138
A boy orphaned in the 1930s by his father's failed use as a foreign power's

362
00:23:10,178 --> 00:23:14,302
political pawn ended up, decades later, as the ceremonial head of a

363
00:23:14,382 --> 00:23:17,585
six-hundred-year dynasty. Nobody planned that outcome.

364
00:23:17,718 --> 00:23:20,467
It's just where the succession chart happened to land.

365
00:23:20,588 --> 00:23:24,952
One branch treated the fall as final and adapted completely, assimilating into

366
00:23:25,009 --> 00:23:29,436
French or Lebanese society, marrying outside the family, building ordinary

367
00:23:29,479 --> 00:23:33,279
professional lives, and largely letting the Ottoman name become a historical

368
00:23:33,341 --> 00:23:35,841
footnote rather than an identity to defend.

369
00:23:36,162 --> 00:23:39,284
A second branch did the opposite. They organized.

370
00:23:39,284 --> 00:23:43,208
Family associations were formed to keep track of every living descendant,

371
00:23:43,261 --> 00:23:47,051
maintain the internal hierarchy of succession according to agnatic seniority

372
00:23:47,119 --> 00:23:51,936
rules going back centuries, and preserve whatever documentation and heirlooms had

373
00:23:51,984 --> 00:23:56,420
made it out of Istanbul. To this day, that succession is tracked

374
00:23:56,452 --> 00:24:00,904
with the same formality a reigning house would use, even though the position

375
00:24:00,968 --> 00:24:05,628
confers no country, no income, and no legal recognition anywhere on Earth.

376
00:24:05,868 --> 00:24:10,753
The clearest example of this divide is Ertuğrul Osman, sometimes called by

377
00:24:10,806 --> 00:24:15,157
journalists "the last Ottoman," who spent most of his adult life in New York,

378
00:24:15,197 --> 00:24:19,241
working an ordinary job in finance and only occasionally acknowledging his

379
00:24:19,291 --> 00:24:20,522
lineage publicly.

380
00:24:20,895 --> 00:24:24,605
He didn't set foot in Turkey until 1992,

381
00:24:24,672 --> 00:24:28,769
sixty-eight years after his family's expulsion, and even then only as a

382
00:24:28,809 --> 00:24:33,333
private citizen, on a passport that gave him no special status whatsoever.

383
00:24:33,509 --> 00:24:36,937
When he died in 2009, Turkish television

384
00:24:36,977 --> 00:24:40,300
covered his funeral in Istanbul as a genuine national event.

385
00:24:40,300 --> 00:24:44,063
The country that exiled the family gave the last direct claimant to the caliphate

386
00:24:44,103 --> 00:24:48,387
a state-adjacent farewell it had refused both his grandfather and Abdulmecid

387
00:24:48,433 --> 00:24:51,430
before him. Not every displaced heir handled it with

388
00:24:51,470 --> 00:24:53,992
Ertuğrul's quiet corporate normalcy, either.

389
00:24:54,132 --> 00:24:58,716
Bayezid Osman, who succeeded him as head of the house after his death in 2009,

390
00:24:58,823 --> 00:25:02,960
spent his life in Istanbul working as a librarian, running puppet shows for

391
00:25:02,991 --> 00:25:06,163
neighborhood children, and looking after his elderly mother.

392
00:25:06,296 --> 00:25:09,846
Acquaintances remembered him mainly for his sense of humor and his enormous

393
00:25:09,910 --> 00:25:14,330
fondness for Ottoman home cooking: stuffed grape leaves, savory pastries,

394
00:25:14,400 --> 00:25:18,014
chicken dishes rich enough that his doctor once told him to cut back, a

395
00:25:18,064 --> 00:25:21,777
request he reportedly ignored, showing up to the follow-up appointment with his

396
00:25:21,825 --> 00:25:24,900
coat pockets stuffed with coins for the vending machine anyway.

397
00:25:25,060 --> 00:25:29,624
His own father, offered the chance to perform publicly as a pianist, turned it

398
00:25:29,688 --> 00:25:33,468
down out of simple stage fright. A family that once commanded an empire

399
00:25:33,521 --> 00:25:37,952
produced, a few generations later, a shy amateur pianist and a librarian who did

400
00:25:38,009 --> 00:25:42,035
puppet shows: proof that exile doesn't just strip away wealth and title.

401
00:25:42,108 --> 00:25:44,878
Eventually it just lets people become ordinary.

402
00:25:44,928 --> 00:25:48,441
Not everyone let go of the principle behind the title even after letting go of

403
00:25:48,477 --> 00:25:51,404
everything else. When Turkey finally lifted the exile in

404
00:25:51,424 --> 00:25:56,128
the mid-1970s, Ertuğrul Osman was working abroad and initially refused to even

405
00:25:56,195 --> 00:25:59,731
apply for the citizenship being offered back to him, on the grounds that he'd

406
00:25:59,779 --> 00:26:03,495
done nothing wrong in the first place and had no need of anyone's amnesty.

407
00:26:03,548 --> 00:26:07,979
He and his second wife, herself a niece of Afghanistan's last king, spent decades

408
00:26:08,027 --> 00:26:11,902
afterward in a modest, rent-controlled Manhattan apartment, paying a few hundred

409
00:26:11,952 --> 00:26:15,025
dollars a month for a home a world away from Topkapı.

410
00:26:15,078 --> 00:26:18,869
He said publicly, more than once, that the republic had been hard on his family

411
00:26:18,929 --> 00:26:23,913
but good for Turkey. His wife was considerably less willing to make peace with history, telling an

412
00:26:23,957 --> 00:26:27,516
American newspaper decades later that the world would be better off if the

413
00:26:27,576 --> 00:26:31,920
caliphate still existed. Two people who'd lived the same exile,

414
00:26:31,960 --> 00:26:35,844
married to each other, still disagreeing to the end about whether any of it had

415
00:26:35,876 --> 00:26:39,047
been worth it. Losing a throne is one thing.

416
00:26:39,097 --> 00:26:42,890
Getting anything back afterward turned out to be almost impossible, and the

417
00:26:42,947 --> 00:26:47,214
family's decades-long attempts to do so form one of the stranger legal footnotes

418
00:26:47,241 --> 00:26:48,976
of twentieth-century monarchy.

419
00:26:49,116 --> 00:26:53,620
The exile law of 1924 didn't just remove the family from the country.

420
00:26:53,727 --> 00:26:57,624
It seized their property outright, and gave them exactly one year to sell

421
00:26:57,660 --> 00:27:01,227
whatever they owned inside Turkey before the state simply took it.

422
00:27:01,440 --> 00:27:04,270
In practice, a year wasn't a real deadline.

423
00:27:04,297 --> 00:27:07,392
It was a fire sale. Family members forced to liquidate

424
00:27:07,472 --> 00:27:12,117
estates, mines, and mansions from outside the country, through agents they barely

425
00:27:12,177 --> 00:27:16,280
had time to vet, ended up handing over land and buildings for a fraction of what

426
00:27:16,307 --> 00:27:20,204
it had been worth just months earlier, or simply signing it over to friends they

427
00:27:20,264 --> 00:27:22,766
trusted to hold it and never got it back from.

428
00:27:22,906 --> 00:27:25,889
The same March 1924 sitting of parliament

429
00:27:25,921 --> 00:27:30,453
that abolished the caliphate also moved every vakıf, the religious endowments

430
00:27:30,469 --> 00:27:34,377
that had funded mosques, schools, and hospitals under the family's authority

431
00:27:34,417 --> 00:27:38,460
for centuries, permanently under state control through a brand-new government

432
00:27:38,513 --> 00:27:42,624
directorate, closing off an entire category of property before any

433
00:27:42,668 --> 00:27:45,106
descendant even had the chance to argue over it.

434
00:27:45,106 --> 00:27:49,831
A 1949 law went further still, specifically blocking any descendant from

435
00:27:49,867 --> 00:27:53,914
claiming inheritance rights over what had been taken, a provision that conveniently

436
00:27:53,957 --> 00:27:58,078
strengthened the hand of neighboring states like Iraq, Syria, and Lebanon in

437
00:27:58,105 --> 00:28:01,521
their own disputes with the family over former Ottoman territory.

438
00:28:01,681 --> 00:28:05,205
The exile itself eased before the property ever did.

439
00:28:05,272 --> 00:28:09,528
Women of the family were allowed to return to Turkey starting in 1952.

440
00:28:09,649 --> 00:28:13,212
Men had to wait until 1974, a gap of over

441
00:28:13,272 --> 00:28:17,536
two decades between the two, and most of the returning men came back to a country

442
00:28:17,568 --> 00:28:20,098
that had changed beyond anything they remembered.

443
00:28:20,205 --> 00:28:24,902
Property was a different story entirely, and it's still being fought over today.

444
00:28:25,035 --> 00:28:27,945
In 2010, descendants of Sultan Abdülhamid

445
00:28:27,965 --> 00:28:31,628
II filed a complaint over roughly 4,200

446
00:28:31,685 --> 00:28:35,632
seized properties, seeking damages reported at close to eighteen billion

447
00:28:35,702 --> 00:28:40,116
dollars. One descendant, speaking to a Turkish newspaper at the time, said the family

448
00:28:40,164 --> 00:28:44,920
wasn't asking for palaces like Topkapı or Dolmabahçe, just land the sultan had

449
00:28:44,971 --> 00:28:49,084
personally held title to, land that had since been sold off or absorbed by the

450
00:28:49,137 --> 00:28:51,246
state's National Estate Institution.

451
00:28:51,366 --> 00:28:55,570
A separate suit followed in 2014, with attorneys for the family citing land

452
00:28:55,623 --> 00:28:59,333
registry documents they'd tracked down in state archives themselves.

453
00:28:59,379 --> 00:29:02,857
Family members have also pointed to a wider precedent they feel Turkey has

454
00:29:02,917 --> 00:29:06,700
ignored: Germany negotiated an actual settlement with its own former royal

455
00:29:06,769 --> 00:29:11,184
family, the Hohenzollerns, returning some property and compensating for the rest, a

456
00:29:11,232 --> 00:29:15,028
deal the Ottomans have spent a century asking for and never received.

457
00:29:15,108 --> 00:29:18,230
Then, in 2025, a century after the exile,

458
00:29:18,284 --> 00:29:22,154
an Istanbul court finally ruled on the underlying question of who counts as an

459
00:29:22,202 --> 00:29:25,117
heir at all. After a legal process that had run for

460
00:29:25,187 --> 00:29:29,841
fifteen years, the court formally recognized seventy-one living descendants

461
00:29:29,894 --> 00:29:32,884
of Abdülhamid II as his legitimate heirs.

462
00:29:33,124 --> 00:29:36,447
It was, on paper, a historic result.

463
00:29:36,517 --> 00:29:40,891
And then the same ruling added the catch: that recognition alone couldn't be used

464
00:29:40,918 --> 00:29:44,654
to automatically transfer any real estate, due to the very restrictions the

465
00:29:44,711 --> 00:29:47,457
family had been fighting since 1949.

466
00:29:47,537 --> 00:29:51,861
Being confirmed as an heir and actually receiving an inheritance turned out to be

467
00:29:51,901 --> 00:29:56,265
two entirely different things. A century after the exile, the family

468
00:29:56,315 --> 00:29:58,827
finally won the argument about who they are.

469
00:29:58,891 --> 00:30:02,430
They still haven't won back anything that argument was actually about.

470
00:30:02,564 --> 00:30:07,155
So who's actually left, and what does being "Ottoman royalty" mean for someone

471
00:30:07,201 --> 00:30:11,879
living right now, in 2026, a hundred years after the exile?

472
00:30:11,999 --> 00:30:15,802
The family estimates its living membership at several hundred people,

473
00:30:15,849 --> 00:30:18,125
spread mostly across Turkey, along with

474
00:30:18,145 --> 00:30:21,888
the United States, France, Lebanon, and Egypt.

475
00:30:21,888 --> 00:30:25,972
The current head of the house holds a position that exists purely as tradition.

476
00:30:25,999 --> 00:30:29,655
No palace, no civil list payment, no state ceremony.

477
00:30:29,755 --> 00:30:33,178
The handover happens through a phone call and an announcement to the family

478
00:30:33,245 --> 00:30:35,981
association, not a coronation, and the

479
00:30:36,045 --> 00:30:40,945
line behind Harun Osman is already mapped out decades in advance, down to men in

480
00:30:40,958 --> 00:30:45,109
their thirties who will likely never hold the title themselves but who know exactly

481
00:30:45,149 --> 00:30:48,072
where they sit if they outlive everyone ahead of them.

482
00:30:48,152 --> 00:30:51,995
So, before we go further, if a story like this is the kind of thing you want more

483
00:30:52,075 --> 00:30:56,799
of, this channel runs on exactly this material, and subscribing is genuinely

484
00:30:56,832 --> 00:31:01,444
what keeps it going. Some descendants engage warmly with Turkish media and historical

485
00:31:01,488 --> 00:31:05,607
commemorations, showing up at documentary premieres or giving interviews about

486
00:31:05,664 --> 00:31:10,492
family history when a Turkish network wants context for a costume drama about

487
00:31:10,519 --> 00:31:13,855
their own ancestors. Others avoid the spotlight entirely,

488
00:31:13,891 --> 00:31:17,778
treating the lineage as a private family matter, an understandable instinct, after

489
00:31:17,810 --> 00:31:20,981
what happened to their grandparents' generation, and a reminder that the same

490
00:31:21,041 --> 00:31:24,905
surname can mean a television career for one cousin and total anonymity for

491
00:31:24,955 --> 00:31:28,828
another, depending entirely on what each of them decided to do with a birthright

492
00:31:28,868 --> 00:31:33,793
neither one chose. What's genuinely unexpected is how the relationship with Turkey itself has

493
00:31:33,855 --> 00:31:36,515
softened. The same state that gave the family a few

494
00:31:36,568 --> 00:31:40,839
hours to leave the country in 1924 now, on occasion, sends official

495
00:31:40,892 --> 00:31:44,843
representation to the funerals of its most prominent surviving descendants, and

496
00:31:44,883 --> 00:31:48,766
its president has personally telephoned the family to offer condolences when a

497
00:31:48,782 --> 00:31:51,729
head of the house has died. It's not restitution.

498
00:31:51,862 --> 00:31:55,412
It's not recognition of any claim. But it's a strange kind of quiet

499
00:31:55,471 --> 00:32:00,457
reconciliation between a republic and the dynasty it replaced, one century, and one

500
00:32:00,510 --> 00:32:04,861
phone call at a time. Harun Osman has been head of the house since 2021.

501
00:32:04,914 --> 00:32:08,224
He won't be the last person to hold that position, and the rules for what happens

502
00:32:08,277 --> 00:32:11,987
after him explain a lot about why this family still functions as a family at

503
00:32:12,047 --> 00:32:14,950
all, a hundred years after losing everything else.

504
00:32:14,990 --> 00:32:19,114
The succession runs on agnatic seniority: the position passes to the oldest living

505
00:32:19,162 --> 00:32:22,076
male descendant in the direct male line, full stop.

506
00:32:22,177 --> 00:32:24,879
Not the oldest child. Not the closest relative.

507
00:32:24,959 --> 00:32:29,123
The oldest man whose father, and his father, and his father before that, trace

508
00:32:29,171 --> 00:32:32,206
back to the House of Osman without a woman anywhere in the chain.

509
00:32:32,262 --> 00:32:35,849
Genealogists maintaining the family's own records count roughly twenty-seven men

510
00:32:35,889 --> 00:32:40,573
currently in that line, spread across Turkey, Lebanon, Egypt, and beyond.

511
00:32:40,653 --> 00:32:44,337
That rule excludes everyone else. Neslişah Sultan's own children and

512
00:32:44,377 --> 00:32:48,100
grandchildren don't count, because Neslişah was a woman: the rule that made

513
00:32:48,140 --> 00:32:51,463
her a Sultan by birth is the same rule that keeps her descendants permanently

514
00:32:51,523 --> 00:32:54,986
outside the line that matters. The same is true for every other female

515
00:32:55,022 --> 00:32:57,388
descendant across four generations of exile.

516
00:32:57,477 --> 00:33:01,152
Hundreds of people who carry Ottoman blood, some of them more publicly

517
00:33:01,203 --> 00:33:05,075
identified with the family name than the men technically ahead of them in line,

518
00:33:05,107 --> 00:33:08,038
have no claim at all under the family's own constitution.

519
00:33:08,262 --> 00:33:11,081
Next after Harun is Osman Selaheddin

520
00:33:11,148 --> 00:33:15,725
Osmanoğlu, born in 1940, descended from Sultan Murad V.

521
00:33:15,805 --> 00:33:19,248
For decades he's chaired the family association that keeps this genealogy

522
00:33:19,298 --> 00:33:23,172
current, the closest thing the Osmanoğlu name has to an institution.

523
00:33:23,412 --> 00:33:26,695
No property comes with the title when it eventually passes to him.

524
00:33:26,855 --> 00:33:30,538
No income, no legal recognition in Turkey or anywhere else.

525
00:33:30,586 --> 00:33:34,302
What he'll inherit is a phone call, an announcement, and the job of being the

526
00:33:34,348 --> 00:33:37,344
person journalists call when something Ottoman is in the news.

527
00:33:37,425 --> 00:33:40,707
That's the real tension inside the family today, and it isn't loud.

528
00:33:40,815 --> 00:33:43,830
It's structural. A rule built for an empire that needed a

529
00:33:43,887 --> 00:33:48,154
single unambiguous heir is still being applied to a family that has nothing left

530
00:33:48,181 --> 00:33:50,877
to inherit except the argument over who's next.

531
00:33:51,010 --> 00:33:54,400
It's worth putting this next to how other fallen dynasties fared in the same

532
00:33:54,460 --> 00:33:56,642
century, because the contrast is stark.

533
00:33:56,742 --> 00:34:00,806
The Romanovs, the last imperial family of Russia, weren't exiled at all.

534
00:34:00,886 --> 00:34:05,530
They were executed by firing squad in a basement in 1918, the entire family

535
00:34:05,574 --> 00:34:09,213
together, with no train ride and no chance to negotiate an exit.

536
00:34:09,273 --> 00:34:13,057
The Habsburgs, who'd ruled over Austria-Hungary, lost the empire itself

537
00:34:13,097 --> 00:34:16,420
but were eventually allowed to keep a portion of their private wealth and,

538
00:34:16,460 --> 00:34:19,783
after decades of legal wrangling in Austria, some family members were

539
00:34:19,823 --> 00:34:23,866
permitted to return as ordinary citizens, without ever fully losing their name's

540
00:34:23,912 --> 00:34:27,710
social standing in parts of Europe. Germany's Hohenzollerns negotiated an

541
00:34:27,767 --> 00:34:31,794
actual settlement with the new republic in the 1920s, trading their claim to

542
00:34:31,840 --> 00:34:35,957
former state property for cash and the right to keep dozens of palaces and

543
00:34:36,027 --> 00:34:40,762
estates, a formal deal the Ottomans never got, and never even had the chance to ask

544
00:34:40,822 --> 00:34:45,646
for. Set against that spectrum, the Ottoman outcome sits closer to the Romanovs' than

545
00:34:45,666 --> 00:34:49,329
the Hohenzollerns'. Nobody put the family in front of a firing squad.

546
00:34:49,430 --> 00:34:53,733
But a hundred and fifty-six people expelled within three days, all assets

547
00:34:53,779 --> 00:34:57,497
seized with no settlement ever negotiated, and a legal system still

548
00:34:57,550 --> 00:35:01,661
refusing full restitution a century later, that's a family that lost,

549
00:35:01,707 --> 00:35:06,465
structurally, almost everything a deposed dynasty can lose without anyone actually

550
00:35:06,505 --> 00:35:09,588
being shot. Strip away the titles, and what's left of

551
00:35:09,648 --> 00:35:13,832
six hundred years of Ottoman rule is a few hundred people scattered across four

552
00:35:13,885 --> 00:35:18,316
or five countries, connected mainly by a shared surname and a voluntary family

553
00:35:18,383 --> 00:35:21,438
association that has no legal power anywhere.

554
00:35:21,438 --> 00:35:25,522
That's the part worth sitting with. Not the collapse itself, empires end,

555
00:35:25,538 --> 00:35:29,366
that's not unusual. It's how completely the ending was allowed to be final.

556
00:35:29,499 --> 00:35:31,848
Twice, for the sultan and the caliph both.

557
00:35:32,008 --> 00:35:35,932
No pension. No palace. No recognition, beyond what individual

558
00:35:35,989 --> 00:35:40,095
family members managed to rebuild for themselves, one ordinary life at a time,

559
00:35:40,122 --> 00:35:45,060
in cities that had never heard of them. Six hundred years built something that looked permanent from the inside.

560
00:35:45,193 --> 00:35:48,022
It took two votes, two years apart, to prove it wasn't.

561
00:35:48,119 --> 00:35:51,866
When the thing you built your whole identity around disappears completely,

562
00:35:51,882 --> 00:35:54,829
what actually survives, and is it ever really the same thing again?

563
00:35:55,042 --> 00:35:58,912
If a story like this pulls at something for you, the idea that certainty like

